A||||2||coyote||Cleaning out a motor, blown head gasket||||5||
Z||000000||coyote||07-19-2001||06:16 PM||||OK guys, what is the best way to clean out the soup in the motor. Tech 4, head is removed, block still in car. Looking for some suggestions.<BR>T.I.A.<P>Coyote||||reg||5
Z||000001||Oreif||07-19-2001||06:25 PM||||Go to Sears and buy a gallon jug of straight 5 weight oil. Remove oil drain plug and pour oil into cylinders. Once the oil has drained out of the cylinders and pan put the drain plug back in and pour more oil in the cylinders. After that has drained turn the engine over by hand a few time then drain the oil pan. You can wipe out any excess oil in the cylinders and once back together it'll smoke a bit but that should get all the water/antifreeze out.<P>------------------<BR>        <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg">      <BR><B><I>Got Emblems?</I></B><P><p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 07-19-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||00lE||07-19-2001||06:45 PM||||If it has been driven for any length of time (ie.. over a few hours) with excessive coolant in the oil, you may as well forget it and just do a rebuild.  Antifreeze is highly corrosive to the material used in the main and rod bearings, and it will cause near immediate damage.  You can count on a bearing failure in the very near future if you disregard this and just attempt a clean up.||||reg||1
